# Retro Arcade — Project Rules

Learning-first C# arcade game: prioritize what a newcomer can read, trace, and change safely over clever or dense code.

## General principles

- Match existing naming, layout, and patterns in the repo.
- Prefer readability, simplicity, and clear names; micro-optimize only in measured hot paths.
- Short, focused methods; extract when a block has one obvious job.
- Remove dead code and duplication; change only what the task needs (no drive-by refactors).

## Documentation

- Brief comments where behavior is non-obvious (timers, difficulty steps, collision edge cases)—not on every line.
- Update **README.md** when features, player-visible behavior, or major systems change.

## Gameplay integrity

- Do **not** change gameplay unless explicitly requested.
- Preserve difficulty balance and progression unless explicitly tuning them.
- Preserve visual feedback hierarchy (e.g. **bomb > generic hit**—do not invert or muddy without intent).

## Performance

- No allocations in hot paths (per-frame update/draw/collision batches): avoid allocating LINQ, string concat in tight loops, boxing, unnecessary closures.
- No `.ToList()` / `.ToArray()` inside per-frame loops; cache or index.
- Cache reused GDI+ objects (pens, brushes, fonts); do not create/dispose every frame without reason.
- No blocking I/O or heavy sync work on the gameplay thread during active play when avoidable.

## Game loop

- Do not silently change fixed-step or delta-time semantics; document timing assumptions if you change them.
- Keep order predictable: input → simulate → collide → render unless a change is deliberate and documented.

## Architecture

- Small, cohesive types; hook into existing systems (collision, spawn, render, audio), not ad-hoc globals.
- Respect UI (forms) vs simulation vs rendering; do not hide game rules only inside paint handlers.

## Data safety

- Persist with safe writes: temp file → flush → **atomic replace** so crashes mid-write do not corrupt the main file.
- Validate reads: size limits, parse errors, safe defaults so bad files cannot break startup or loops.

## Configuration

- No unexplained magic numbers; use centralized config types/constants already in the project.
- New tunables go through the same configuration structure.

## Visual consistency

- Follow existing palette, scale, HUD, and effects; keep retro/pixel-consistent choices unless redesign is requested.

## Testing

- Add or extend tests for regressable behavior (collision, scoring, difficulty, persistence).
- Tests must be deterministic; avoid flaky timers, shared real filesystem, or order-dependent races unless isolated.

## Code quality

- Meaningful names; small methods; no dead branches; extract shared helpers when two places encode the same rule.

## State management

- Phase changes (menu, play, pause, game over) stay explicit and go through existing state APIs—no hidden transitions from deep call chains.

## Audio

- Non-blocking for the frame loop; follow existing one-shot vs loop patterns; no blocking audio init every frame.

## Practical boundaries

- Skip enterprise-only patterns (heavy DI, DDD layers) unless asked. Keep rules actionable for this codebase.
